Subject: Read an ASCII character of a large character set with a bitmap

A lexer state that tests a large character set does a binary search of the ranges of the set. The
set of the identifier characters of Unicode has 687 ranges, so the search does approximately ten
steps. The parser does that search for each letter of each name of each source file. Nearly every
one of those letters is an ASCII character.

This patch writes a bitmap of the code points 0 to 127 beside the ranges of each large character
set. The new runtime function `set_contains_ascii` reads one bit of that bitmap for a code point
less than 128. It calls `set_contains` for the other code points, so the result does not change.

The bitmap uses 16 bytes for each large character set that a parser reads. The C++ grammar of the
tree-sitter-cpp fork has two of them, so the tables use 32 bytes more.

MEASUREMENTS. 6000 files of the C++ corpus of that fork, with one parser in each run, and with the
same grammar before and after the patch:

- `set_contains` takes 3.17% of the cycles before the patch. `set_contains_ascii` takes 0.34%
  after it.
- The instruction count of the full parse decreases by 2.18%, the cycle count by 3.04%, and the
  branch misses by 3.11%.

The gain is a minimum. The generated parser.c carries `#pragma GCC optimize ("O0")`, so the
compiler keeps both functions as calls and inlines neither one.

CORRECTNESS. The trees of 329,387 corpus files and of 7,646 test files do not change, and no tree
hash changes. The parse operation count reads 1.00000. The inputs below also give the same trees,
and the corpus reaches none of them, because it holds no identifier with a character above 127:

- An identifier with a letter above 127, at the start of the name and inside it
- The universal character names `\u00e9` and `\U000000e9`
- The code point 127 and the code point 128, alone and inside a name
- A user-defined literal suffix with a letter above 127, which reads the second character set.

The comments hold the marker "(tree-sitter-cpp fork)". Remove the marker before you send the
patch.

--- a/lib/src/parser.h	2026-09-16 05:18:38.115099506 +0200
+++ b/lib/src/parser.h	2026-09-16 05:18:45.239534464 +0200
@@ -169,6 +169,27 @@
   return (lookahead >= range->start && lookahead <= range->end);
 }
 
+// True when a character set holds the character (tree-sitter-cpp fork).
+//
+// `ascii` holds one bit for each of the code points 0 to 127, and the generator writes it beside the
+// ranges of the set. Nearly each character of a source is in that range, and the lexer then reads
+// one bit in the place of a binary search of the ranges. A set of the identifier characters of
+// Unicode has 687 ranges, so the search takes ten steps for each letter of each name.
+//
+// A lookahead that is not in the range 0 to 127, and a lookahead that is negative, take the search.
+static inline bool set_contains_ascii(
+  const TSCharacterRange *ranges,
+  uint32_t len,
+  const uint64_t *ascii,
+  int32_t lookahead
+) {
+  uint32_t code_point = (uint32_t)lookahead;
+  if (code_point < 128) {
+    return (ascii[code_point >> 6] >> (code_point & 63)) & 1;
+  }
+  return set_contains(ranges, len, lookahead);
+}
+
